 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Gate drive",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "The channel begins to conduct with the gate between -1.0 V and -3.0 V from the source (at -250 µA with gate tied to drain). On-resistance is specified at two drives: 340 mΩ max at -10 V and 550 mΩ max at -4.5 V. At -4.5 V the on-resistance climbs steeply once drain current passes about 4 A (Fig.3), while at -10 V it stays near 0.3 Ω up to 8 A. Gate leakage is at most ±100 nA at ±20 V, and the gate charges to -10 V with 5.4 nC typical, the Miller plateau sitting near -3.6 V (Fig.6)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Heat and current",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The continuous current and pulsed current ratings assume the part is on an FR4 board and hold for t ≤ 5 s. Steady-state junction-to-ambient resistance is 357 °C/W; dissipation is 1.25 W at 25 °C ambient, and continuous drain current falls from -1.25 A at 25 °C to -0.85 A at 70 °C. Junction and storage temperature range is -55 °C to +150 °C. Normalized on-resistance rises linearly with temperature, from about 0.63 at -50 °C to about 1.86 at 150 °C (Fig.4). The transient impedance curves of Fig.7 are normalized to 130 °C/W."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Safe operating area",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Fig.8 is drawn for a 150 °C junction limit at 25 °C ambient. It is bounded by the on-resistance line at low voltage, by -8 A of drain current and by 60 V of drain voltage, with separate power limits for pulses of 10 µs, 100 µs, 1 ms, 10 ms and 100 ms; DC, 100 s, 10 s and 1 s share one boundary."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Body diode",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "The body diode, anode at the drain and cathode at the source, carries up to -1.25 A continuously, with -0.82 V typical and -1.2 V maximum forward drop at -1.25 A. Reverse recovery takes 30 ns typical, 55 ns maximum, at -1.25 A and 100 A/µs."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Switching",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Switching times are taken with -4.5 V gate drive from a 6.0 Ω generator into a 3.0 Ω load at -30 V and -1.0 A: about 10.5 ns delay and 11.5 ns rise at turn-on, 15.5 ns delay and 7.5 ns fall at turn-off. Capacitances fall as drain voltage rises; Coss starts near 257 pF at 0 V (Fig.5) and is 32 pF typical at -30 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Uses",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Named for battery-powered equipment, solid-state relays and direct interfacing with TTL or CMOS logic, as a voltage-controlled small-signal switch."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Construction",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Mold compound rated UL 94V-0; the part is RoHS compliant and halogen-free. Leads are solder plated and pass the solderability test of MIL-STD-750 Method 2026. Marking on the body is S9."
  }
 ],
